1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,840
They're fast, sleek, and sexy.

2
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:07,840
They're built to quietly maneuver

3
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:10,540
in the shadows of covert operations

4
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:14,140
or to wow audiences on the big screen.

5
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:17,660
Theirs is a realm that lies between fact and fiction,

6
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:19,960
where the imaginations of gadget masters

7
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:22,060
can literally save the world.

8
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:26,060
The Gadgets of James Bond, next on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
To be a good spy requires courage, daring,

10
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:50,360
and the best toys technology can offer.

11
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:52,860
So it's no surprise the world of spymasters

12
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:54,820
is the fodder for filmmakers.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,500
Real-life spy gadgets often inspire

14
00:01:02,501 --> 00:01:05,300
the bewildering array of props and gizmos

15
00:01:05,301 --> 00:01:07,900
created by magicians of the movies,

16
00:01:07,901 --> 00:01:12,840
a celluloid version of art imitating life.

17
00:01:12,841 --> 00:01:14,780
Gadgets are so important.

18
00:01:14,781 --> 00:01:17,280
Every preview I've been to, everybody I've talked to,

19
00:01:17,281 --> 00:01:18,920
on the show, what gadgets have you got this time?

20
00:01:18,921 --> 00:01:22,620
Everybody is madly interested in gadgets.

21
00:01:22,621 --> 00:01:24,360
The audience loved them.

22
00:01:24,361 --> 00:01:27,260
There was one film where we got a real audience response

23
00:01:27,261 --> 00:01:28,820
that they really wanted more gadgets.

24
00:01:28,821 --> 00:01:32,700
And so it's something we take into consideration hugely

25
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:34,660
when we're embarking on a film that we've

26
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:37,400
got to do these gadgets, you know, to keep them happy.

27
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:38,760
Because they just love it.

28
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:41,060
And we love it as well.

29
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:43,760
Conversely, gadgets found in films

30
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:47,480
often wind up being the genesis behind real world spy

31
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:50,280
technology.

32
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,640
This briefcase basically is the same theme

33
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:54,640
as from Russia With Love.

34
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:58,280
Spy toys can be divided into several categories.

35
00:01:58,281 --> 00:02:07,720
cars, boats, guns, covert spy tools, and flying machines.

36
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:17,440
One of the best known spy gadgets used in films and television

37
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:20,140
shows was the Bell Textron rocket belt.

38
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:26,780
The belt and later versions of the flying machine

39
00:02:26,781 --> 00:02:33,020
are thrust-driven devices that can literally make a person fly.

40
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:36,420
I think if I had to pick a favorite thing that

41
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:38,560
is believable, that I would love to have,

42
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:41,200
I think it's the rocket pack on the back.

43
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:43,360
I think that was a wonderful, wonderful gadget

44
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:46,140
because it was real, it was futuristic and unbelievable.

45
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:48,000
You look at it and you think, wow, is that on wires?

46
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:49,440
And you actually realize it's for real.

47
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:51,200
I think that was very innovative in its day.

48
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:53,400
I think it's fantastic.

49
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,480
Wendell Moore, a Bell Aerospace Engineer

50
00:02:56,481 --> 00:02:59,880
in Buffalo, New York, created the belt in the late 1950s

51
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:01,180
and early 1960s.

52
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:08,020
Moore got the idea while working with liquid hydrogen

53
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:11,760
peroxide used in the rocket boosters of high-flying research

54
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:15,700
aircraft to keep them stable at stratospheric altitudes.

55
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:22,200
The first tests of the belt were done with pilots tethered to

56
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:24,300
lines inside an airplane hangar.

57
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:28,620
By converting the liquid to a gas,

58
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:31,820
a tremendous amount of thrust was created.

59
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:38,420
Enough thrust, Moore realized, to launch a man into the air.

60
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:41,520
By 1962, Moore perfected the belt.

61
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:45,640
He made tanks that could hold up to 50 pounds of fuel.

62
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:48,540
He created easy to use pitch and yaw controls

63
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,840
on the handles of the belt.

64
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:54,640
He installed a warning system inside the helmet

65
00:03:54,641 --> 00:03:59,140
to let a pilot know his fuel was running low.

66
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:02,240
Moore's belt allowed a man to fly for up to 20 seconds

67
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:06,120
at 60 miles per hour and land safely.

68
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:12,760
Hugh Neeson worked with Moore on the project.

69
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:16,100
It turns out it was all a very natural sequence.

70
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:18,500
If you turn this left hand, you rotate it.

71
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:20,740
If you turn this hand, you went up higher.

72
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:23,160
If you push both back, you went forward.

73
00:04:23,161 --> 00:04:26,400
If you lift it up, you slow down.

74
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:29,640
So it turned out to be elegantly simple.

75
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:31,440
In fact, I think the early criteria

76
00:04:31,540 --> 00:04:35,100
was have people who weren't pilots fly it.

77
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,780
The original thought was to supply this to the Army,

78
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:41,380
to ordinary troops, far out vision, if you will.

79
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:44,480
And they did not want to constrain by saying it was so complex

80
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:45,880
that you had to have a pilot.

81
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:52,120
In 1963, Bill Suter was a 19-year-old neighbor

82
00:04:52,121 --> 00:04:56,400
neighbor of William Moore's who was looking for a summer job.

83
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:59,220
What he got was work as one of the first pilots

84
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:03,200
to strap on the rocket belt.

85
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:04,700
The second day that I showed up,

86
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:09,100
I was given a low pressure tryout on the tether.

87
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:11,640
And when they keep the fuel pressure low,

88
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:13,500
there's not enough power to get you off the ground.

89
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:16,940
But it will shove you around so you get a feel for what

90
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:18,120
you're doing.

91
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:19,080
And the first couple of attempts in the hangar

92
00:05:19,081 --> 00:05:22,420
are with low pressure, so you can feel the reaction

93
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:24,760
and what you have to do to overcome it.

94
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:27,420
But then when you get full pressure and your feet leave

95
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:30,420
the ground, everything you thought about previously

96
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:31,360
is gone.

97
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:33,820
It's as soon as your feet are off the ground

98
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:37,460
and you're riding on that thing, it's entirely different from

99
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:40,740
anything you ever experienced.

100
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:44,040
Sutter went on to become an expert belt pilot.

101
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:47,200
He flew at demonstrations all over the world

102
00:05:47,201 --> 00:05:51,200
and was even hired to fly in the James Bond film, Thunderball.

103
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:56,200
I'm one lucky son of a gun.

104
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:59,440
I was lucky that Wendell hired me,

105
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:04,080
and I was lucky I made it to all those 40-some foreign countries

106
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:09,720
and all over the world, met some incredible people.

107
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:12,720
It was an experience of a lifetime, I guess,

108
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,720
you know, that went on for 30-some years.

109
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:20,500
At a private airfield outside of Dallas, Texas,

110
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:23,040
today's version of James Bond's rocket belt

111
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:24,240
takes to the skies.

112
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:30,240
A company called Powerhouse Productions owns the belt,

113
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:33,580
the only one in existence that still flies.

114
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:40,060
Powerhouse owner Kenny Gibson has flown the belt hundreds

115
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:42,860
of times in exhibitions all over the world,

116
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:47,200
from television shows to the Super Bowl.

117
00:06:47,201 --> 00:06:51,200
I flew several years ago at the Super Bowl,

118
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:53,200
and when I landed, you would have thought

119
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:55,940
I just made the winning touchdown at the Super Bowl.

120
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:57,540
I mean, the people came out of their seat,

121
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:00,840
they threw their arms up, and it was just this big roar.

122
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:03,720
When you get face to face with them to talk about it,

123
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:06,860
they're just amazed that a man can fly this way.

124
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,860
Even though this has been around for 35 years,

125
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:12,800
they're still amazed that this thing flies

126
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:15,800
in the capacity that it flies in.

127
00:07:15,801 --> 00:07:19,440
In all his flights, which can reach up to 200 feet

128
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:22,900
off the ground, Gibson said one question is asked more

129
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,340
than all the others combined.

130
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:28,740
What does it feel like to fly?

131
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:30,640
The best thing I can come up with is it's

132
00:07:30,740 --> 00:07:33,660
like you would imagine flying on a magic carpet.

133
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:36,360
You're just cruising around really smooth

134
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:39,760
and just making turns very graceful and very easy.

135
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:48,740
After two decades of flying the belt throughout the world

136
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:52,040
at venues big and small, Kinney has retired.

137
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:59,880
But at the powerhouse field today,

138
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:04,380
pilot Eric Scott will demonstrate how the belt works.

139
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,280
In the business of stunt flying,

140
00:08:06,380 --> 00:08:08,820
Scott is known as the Rocket Man.

141
00:08:11,420 --> 00:08:14,760
The ground team makes some final adjustments to the belt.

142
00:08:14,761 --> 00:08:17,600
Then it's time for the rocket man to take a test flight.

143
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:25,100
Scott slowly lifts off the ground and hovers for 10 seconds.

144
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:27,600
Everything with the belt appears to be working fine.

145
00:08:34,420 --> 00:08:37,420
After refueling, Scott is ready to take to the skies.

146
00:08:37,421 --> 00:08:38,320
skies.

147
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:46,520
Scott flies above a nearby treeline.

148
00:08:46,620 --> 00:09:00,620
He makes a circle in the sky and lands at the same spot

149
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:02,320
from where he took off.

150
00:09:02,321 --> 00:09:14,260
I was just having a little fun up there.

151
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:17,240
I didn't see it.

152
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:19,800
Oh, that's kind of fun.

153
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:22,200
Yeah, it was smooth.

154
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:23,060
It was nice.

155
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:23,860
Good job.

156
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:25,140
What's the time?

157
00:09:25,141 --> 00:09:25,841
Thanks, everybody.

158
00:09:25,842 --> 00:09:26,542
Hey, good job.

159
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:27,800
28 seconds.

160
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:28,620
Beautiful.

161
00:09:28,621 --> 00:09:29,321
28.

162
00:09:29,322 --> 00:09:30,022
Beautiful.

163
00:09:30,023 --> 00:09:30,723
Perfect.

164
00:09:30,724 --> 00:09:32,880
Even though they've seen it hundreds of times,

165
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:38,020
every flight for the powerhouse crew is still special.

166
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:39,320
You can't explain the feeling.

167
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:40,320
It's like a dream.

168
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:41,520
It's reality.

169
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:44,400
It's just one of those wonderful, hard-to-explain feelings

170
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:47,000
that everybody probably wishes they could have the opportunity

171
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:47,900
to do.

172
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:07,380
When we continue, an eccentric English inventor creates a bizarre flying machine and amazes

173
00:10:07,381 --> 00:10:15,300
moviegoers like never before.

174
00:10:15,301 --> 00:10:22,100
To operate his gadgets, the character of 007 was required to master firearms, knife throwing,

175
00:10:22,101 --> 00:10:29,460
high speed driving, scuba diving, rock climbing and martial arts.

176
00:10:29,461 --> 00:10:32,960
James Bond Gadgets will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

177
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:43,000
We now return to James Bond Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

178
00:10:43,001 --> 00:10:47,020
Two hours north of London in a rolling green field just

179
00:10:47,021 --> 00:10:51,320
outside his country manor, 85-year-old Ken Wallace

180
00:10:51,321 --> 00:10:54,060
prepares to defy gravity.

181
00:10:54,061 --> 00:10:57,220
The retired British aviator and inventor

182
00:10:57,221 --> 00:10:59,320
of his most famous creation.

183
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:04,420
Though it looks like a helicopter,

184
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:09,560
it's actually called an auto gyro and flies like an airplane.

185
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:13,460
Its rear rotor creates the thrust needed to stay aloft.

186
00:11:13,461 --> 00:11:14,960
to stay aloft.

187
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:30,580
I used to ride motorbikes at full northern lots of times.

188
00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:32,340
I love speedboat racing.

189
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:34,540
I've loved flying and aeroplanes.

190
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:36,780
But I love these things more than any other

191
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:39,960
because there's no such precise way of going flying that I know.

192
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:41,520
And you get the feeling of flying,

193
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:43,060
especially when you're up in the open,

194
00:11:43,061 --> 00:11:44,300
and so on.

195
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:49,460
It really is, in my opinion, the best way of all to go flying.

196
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:54,040
In 1967, Wallace flew an auto gyro named Little Nelly

197
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:58,740
in the James Bond film You Only Live Twice.

198
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:01,040
The gadget was a flying fantasy.

199
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:03,840
It wowed audiences and made Wallace and Little Nelly

200
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:05,120
famous throughout the world.

201
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:10,360
It's entirely a chance the way it came about.

202
00:12:10,361 --> 00:12:13,600
Little Nelly was the chorus girl that became the star.

203
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:22,400
Since 1961, Wallace has built 19 auto gyros, each

204
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:26,780
with their own strength and flight characteristics.

205
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,680
Well, we're in the politely called hangar,

206
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:35,520
where I have 19 auto gyros of my design, of which 18 would

207
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:38,280
literally fire up and fly straight away, including,

208
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:39,420
of course, little Nelly.

209
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:44,940
How an autogyro flies is based on simple science.

210
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:48,740
Wallace describes it as two sycamore seeds working

211
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:50,440
in unison to stay aloft.

212
00:12:54,380 --> 00:12:57,480
We all know that a sycamore seed is a little winged seed.

213
00:12:57,580 --> 00:12:59,840
And when it falls from the tree, it whirls around,

214
00:12:59,940 --> 00:13:03,520
and it falls much more slowly than if it didn't have

215
00:13:03,620 --> 00:13:04,680
that little wing on.

216
00:13:04,780 --> 00:13:08,520
Now, the autogyro I'm flying is, in effect,

217
00:13:08,521 --> 00:13:12,360
like a pair of sycamore seeds joined together at the root.

218
00:13:12,460 --> 00:13:16,220
When you have an engine and a propeller and push it along,

219
00:13:16,320 --> 00:13:19,060
if you set the angle of the rotors

220
00:13:19,160 --> 00:13:21,560
so that they're tilted up at the front,

221
00:13:21,660 --> 00:13:24,840
you can actually stay up and climb.

222
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:28,180
It's quite an incredible thing that it works as well as it does,

223
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:31,080
and it feels so right when you're flying it.

224
00:13:31,180 --> 00:13:33,780
Wallace has invented all kinds of gadgets

225
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:37,480
in the past 50 years, but it is the auto gyro

226
00:13:37,481 --> 00:13:41,320
that brings him the most joy.

227
00:13:41,420 --> 00:13:45,720
I first flew my own design entirely in 1961,

228
00:13:45,820 --> 00:13:49,220
and I've got a heck of a lot of confidence in them since then.

229
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:52,860
All the machines in my shed are basically the same design,

230
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:55,200
but they are quite different in nature

231
00:13:55,300 --> 00:13:57,600
because of the nature of the engine that's used.

232
00:13:57,700 --> 00:13:59,660
Some are two-seaters, some are single-seaters.

233
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:02,340
There's one fully enclosed single-seater

234
00:14:02,440 --> 00:14:05,100
with 130 horsepower Rolls-Royce engine.

235
00:14:05,101 --> 00:14:07,140
But I must confess, I like the open ones

236
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:09,500
rather than the fully enclosed ones.

237
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:12,800
He says the aircraft is the ultimate spy toy

238
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:17,240
because it is small, lightweight, relatively quiet,

239
00:14:17,340 --> 00:14:21,180
and can fly over an enemy undetected.

240
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:23,820
If you need a helicopter, you've got to have a helicopter.

241
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:26,860
But if you want to fly low and slow and not disturb the ground

242
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:30,500
or fly very low over crops, searching for people

243
00:14:30,600 --> 00:14:33,860
and that sort of thing, not do a scruff of damage, the load.

244
00:14:33,861 --> 00:14:36,900
Though auto gyros are lightweight and small compared

245
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,300
to conventional aircraft, Wallace said they're very safe.

246
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:47,340
In the 40 years Wallace has been flying auto gyros,

247
00:14:47,440 --> 00:14:50,080
he's had only a handful of engine problems.

248
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:56,620
Because the aircraft always flies in auto rotation,

249
00:14:56,720 --> 00:14:59,620
the same dynamic used by helicopter pilots

250
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:01,380
if their engines fail.

251
00:15:01,381 --> 00:15:04,980
Wallace has always landed safely.

252
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:06,820
I think it's one of the safest aircraft

253
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:08,820
there should be to fly, actually.

254
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:10,820
I completely believe in them, which

255
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:14,720
is why I spent so many years on them and so many variations.

256
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:15,820
I fly them at night.

257
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:17,900
I fly them with infrared line scan on board,

258
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,300
transmitting to the ground all the time in military trials

259
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:21,340
and exercises.

260
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:23,400
All weather has a landing light on it,

261
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:26,000
land it with its own landing light at night.

262
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:27,840
And of course, it can taxi on the ground,

263
00:15:27,940 --> 00:15:30,680
taxi through the woods into a critical point or something

264
00:15:30,681 --> 00:15:32,720
or something, whereas a helicopter has to stand

265
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:34,280
outside the woods.

266
00:15:34,380 --> 00:15:37,780
So it's a thoroughly practical aircraft.

267
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:41,020
Little Nelly, Wallace's most famous auto gyro,

268
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:45,520
has done duty in films and television shows.

269
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:47,560
To make the aircraft more menacing,

270
00:15:47,660 --> 00:15:50,440
movie makers have outfitted Little Nelly with a battery

271
00:15:50,540 --> 00:15:54,340
of rockets and machine guns, some of which actually fire.

272
00:15:54,341 --> 00:15:59,640
And though Wallace is proud of his auto gyro's work

273
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:03,320
in the entertainment field, its true accomplishments

274
00:16:03,420 --> 00:16:06,880
came closer to the spy game.

275
00:16:06,980 --> 00:16:09,860
The British military and several police agencies

276
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:12,920
have put a Wallace auto gyro to good use,

277
00:16:13,020 --> 00:16:16,800
either spying on enemy troops or helping solve crimes.

278
00:16:19,940 --> 00:16:24,340
This one is a prototype I made with a full ink coat.

279
00:16:24,341 --> 00:16:27,940
It has a 130-horsepower Rolls-Royce aero engine in it.

280
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:29,840
It's the second prototype engine they made.

281
00:16:29,940 --> 00:16:33,540
But it also has a pack of four aerial cameras.

282
00:16:33,640 --> 00:16:37,280
And they were, first of all, used for coastal ecology research.

283
00:16:37,380 --> 00:16:40,720
But then it led, that led to the detection of buried bodies

284
00:16:40,820 --> 00:16:42,920
as of murder victims for the police.

285
00:16:43,020 --> 00:16:46,100
I did over 350 flights on test work,

286
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:48,940
detecting where bodies were buried.

287
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:52,060
Besides finding bodies, the British military

288
00:16:52,061 --> 00:16:55,260
The British military has used an auto gyro in the skies

289
00:16:55,360 --> 00:16:57,960
over Europe to assist in everything from troop

290
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:03,000
reconnaissance to NATO training exercises.

291
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:05,000
It was all very real.

292
00:17:05,100 --> 00:17:08,480
And in fact, the sister of Little Nelly has been updated

293
00:17:08,580 --> 00:17:10,220
and back in military covers.

294
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:13,080
And she's taken part in aerial quick deployment exercises

295
00:17:13,180 --> 00:17:16,320
and out in NATO bases in Bavaria and the Lake Constance

296
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:17,380
Battle Area.

297
00:17:17,480 --> 00:17:20,280
So Little Nelly may have been a gimmick for the film,

298
00:17:20,281 --> 00:17:21,980
but she's also for real.

299
00:17:30,020 --> 00:17:33,420
Coming up, a high-powered speedboat built in Europe

300
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:36,760
for the movies makes a splash in an Illinois river.

301
00:17:42,660 --> 00:17:46,300
In the late 1970s, a Scottish exploration team

302
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:50,280
fitted a Ken Wallace auto gyro with infrared cameras.

303
00:17:50,281 --> 00:17:53,520
They use the aircraft in an unsuccessful search

304
00:17:53,620 --> 00:17:55,920
for the Loch Ness Monster.

305
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:59,920
James Bond Gadgets will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

306
00:18:04,020 --> 00:18:10,440
We now return to James Bond Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

307
00:18:10,540 --> 00:18:13,800
Inside an unassuming steel shed in the middle

308
00:18:13,900 --> 00:18:16,740
of an Illinois cornfield is parked one of the most

309
00:18:16,741 --> 00:18:22,180
of the most remarkable water-going spy toys ever created,

310
00:18:22,280 --> 00:18:26,120
the Q-boat, and the world is not enough.

311
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:29,280
The boat is a prized possession of the Ian Fleming Foundation

312
00:18:29,380 --> 00:18:32,660
and its vice president, Doug Redanius, a lifelong bond

313
00:18:32,760 --> 00:18:35,860
enthusiast.

314
00:18:35,960 --> 00:18:38,060
Redanius has agreed to take the boat

315
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:43,940
to the nearby Kankakee River to demonstrate its power.

316
00:18:43,941 --> 00:18:46,280
It's only the second time the Q-boat,

317
00:18:46,380 --> 00:18:48,420
named after its onscreen inventor,

318
00:18:48,520 --> 00:18:52,420
has been in the water since it was used in the 1999 film.

319
00:18:58,220 --> 00:19:01,460
One of the interesting things about transporting the Q-boat

320
00:19:01,560 --> 00:19:03,920
is it's very difficult to cover.

321
00:19:04,020 --> 00:19:07,200
We haven't had a cover made specifically for it

322
00:19:07,300 --> 00:19:08,860
because it's an odd shape.

323
00:19:08,861 --> 00:19:13,360
So that tends to lead to transporting it

324
00:19:13,460 --> 00:19:17,200
exposed to the elements, which means many times I'll

325
00:19:17,300 --> 00:19:20,400
look in the rear view mirror and all of a sudden have a caravan

326
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:23,040
of people who recognize the boat.

327
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:27,120
They'll follow you into a rest area or to a filling station

328
00:19:27,220 --> 00:19:28,520
and want to take photographs.

329
00:19:28,620 --> 00:19:33,220
So a journey of 25 miles pulling the Q boat can take hours

330
00:19:33,320 --> 00:19:35,220
because people want to get their picture taken with it.

331
00:19:35,320 --> 00:19:38,120
It's very recognizable.

332
00:19:38,121 --> 00:19:41,820
Though boats are a common sight on this section of the river,

333
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:45,060
no one here has ever seen anything like the Q boat

334
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:45,860
being launched.

335
00:19:50,660 --> 00:19:54,700
The Q boat that we have is the stunt version of the boat.

336
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:58,900
It does have what's called a complete Q dashboard in it.

337
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,720
So you're going to see all the elaborate buttons and displays.

338
00:20:02,820 --> 00:20:06,180
It looks like the cockpit of the space shuttle.

339
00:20:06,181 --> 00:20:11,520
An onboard computer monitors the boat's propulsion system.

340
00:20:11,620 --> 00:20:13,580
In the film, the computer was programmed

341
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:16,220
to control the craft's vast array of weapons.

342
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:29,340
In the waters of the Kankakee River,

343
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:35,380
the boat easily reaches speeds over 80 miles an hour.

344
00:20:35,381 --> 00:20:44,640
The Q-boat actually has a 350 horsepower Chevrolet V8 engine in it, and it has a very simple 14-foot aluminum hull.

345
00:20:45,540 --> 00:20:49,880
The upper deck, all the special effects, the shrouding and everything, is all fiberglass.

346
00:20:49,881 --> 00:20:53,920
So there's not much weight for a boat with 350 horsepower.

347
00:20:54,500 --> 00:20:58,140
It's gas pedal fed, so you drive it like a car.

348
00:20:58,360 --> 00:21:00,220
The only thing is there's no brake pedal.

349
00:21:00,420 --> 00:21:03,880
You have to be constantly monitoring the forward and reverse.

350
00:21:03,881 --> 00:21:06,680
But as anyone knows, on the water,

351
00:21:06,780 --> 00:21:09,560
25, 30 miles an hour is very fast.

352
00:21:09,660 --> 00:21:12,520
This boat is capable of going three times that fast.

353
00:21:15,420 --> 00:21:19,100
So it actually becomes quite scary when you drive this boat

354
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:21,660
on the water.

355
00:21:21,760 --> 00:21:24,100
The boat was built half a world away

356
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:27,800
at London's Pinewood Studios under the supervision

357
00:21:27,900 --> 00:21:30,980
of Eon Productions Art Department and Special Effects

358
00:21:30,981 --> 00:21:32,680
coordinator Chris Korbold.

359
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:37,680
The gadgets on it were constructed here.

360
00:21:37,780 --> 00:21:41,060
We put in missiles.

361
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:43,220
There originally were guns in it, which

362
00:21:43,320 --> 00:21:45,420
were never shown in the film.

363
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:47,100
The jets that were coming out the back,

364
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:50,560
we built versions that went across the land,

365
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:54,240
you know, for where it took the shortcut.

366
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:55,900
It was quite a mean boat, actually.

367
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:00,980
It was, it could certainly leave your hair standing on end.

368
00:22:00,981 --> 00:22:04,780
The waters in this part of the river are very shallow,

369
00:22:04,880 --> 00:22:08,760
only six inches deep in some places.

370
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:11,560
But because of the way the Q-boat was constructed,

371
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:14,700
with its interior engine and small hull,

372
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:17,760
it can easily move in extremely shallow depths.

373
00:22:23,060 --> 00:22:27,040
Besides the Q-boat, the archive section of Doug Radanias' Ian

374
00:22:27,140 --> 00:22:30,080
Fleming Foundation has collected a dozen other vehicles

375
00:22:30,081 --> 00:22:33,080
from the Bond films.

376
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:37,980
There's the Glastron speedboat from the 1979 film Moonraker,

377
00:22:38,080 --> 00:22:40,220
dubbed the Aston Martin of the waves

378
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:43,760
because it had an escape parasail for pedo system

379
00:22:43,860 --> 00:22:46,560
and mine deployment.

380
00:22:46,660 --> 00:22:52,100
There's the submarine from 1981's For Your Eyes Only.

381
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:55,800
And the Parahawk flying snowmobiles from The World

382
00:22:55,900 --> 00:22:58,800
Is Not Enough.

383
00:22:58,801 --> 00:23:02,000
But it's the collection inside Rodinius' home

384
00:23:02,001 --> 00:23:03,840
that is most stunning.

385
00:23:03,841 --> 00:23:06,400
He has one of the world's most extensive ensembles

386
00:23:06,401 --> 00:23:10,180
of spy toys and several Bond gadgets on display.

387
00:23:13,440 --> 00:23:17,560
This is the wrist dart gun from the 1979 James Bond film

388
00:23:17,561 --> 00:23:18,980
Moonraker.

389
00:23:18,981 --> 00:23:22,260
It's supposedly activated by nerve impulse

390
00:23:22,261 --> 00:23:27,060
from one's nerves in the inside of their wrist.

391
00:23:27,061 --> 00:23:33,060
James Bond uses this in the office when he's given it by the cue master.

392
00:23:33,380 --> 00:23:36,620
And he shoots a dart unknowingly into a priceless painting.

393
00:23:36,920 --> 00:23:41,140
So it's one of those gadgets that you have to be very careful how you use it

394
00:23:41,141 --> 00:23:43,620
so you don't shoot the person you're shaking hands with.

395
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,060
This item is a limpet mine.

396
00:23:46,360 --> 00:23:47,100
It's magnetic.

397
00:23:47,640 --> 00:23:50,900
Once it's activated and is attached to its intended target,

398
00:23:51,140 --> 00:23:54,840
one has only a few moments to get away before it explodes.

399
00:23:54,841 --> 00:24:01,120
This is a rebreather from Thunderball from the 1965 James Bond film.

400
00:24:01,440 --> 00:24:05,500
It was used by 007 to escape from a shark tank.

401
00:24:06,280 --> 00:24:10,800
He was thrown into a swimming pool, which was capped off with a roof he couldn't get out.

402
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:14,720
So he reached in his pocket, took it out, stuck it in his mouth,

403
00:24:15,500 --> 00:24:21,180
and wanted the audience to believe that he could survive.

404
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:23,580
Unfortunately, it didn't work.

405
00:24:23,581 --> 00:24:28,580
but various military organizations around the country were fooled.

406
00:24:28,581 --> 00:24:32,580
I was sitting in the office one day, and the phone went,

407
00:24:32,581 --> 00:24:37,580
and it was a gentleman from the Royal Engineers or something like that,

408
00:24:37,581 --> 00:24:43,580
and he said, could you tell me anything about the miniaturized underwater breathing apparatus?

409
00:24:43,581 --> 00:24:45,580
So I said, yeah, I'm quite au fait with it.

410
00:24:45,581 --> 00:24:50,580
So he said, what we'd really like to know, how long could you stay underwater with it?

411
00:24:50,581 --> 00:24:51,580
with it.

412
00:24:51,581 --> 00:24:53,280
So I said, as long as you could hold your breath.

413
00:24:53,281 --> 00:24:55,980
And I can imagine this poor fellow going absolutely white.

414
00:24:55,981 --> 00:24:56,980
He said, what do you mean?

415
00:24:56,981 --> 00:24:58,880
I said, it's a cod up.

416
00:24:58,881 --> 00:25:00,820
But he said, Bond was underwater for about three

417
00:25:00,821 --> 00:25:01,920
or four minutes.

418
00:25:01,921 --> 00:25:06,120
I said, yeah, but that is the expertise of the editor.

419
00:25:06,121 --> 00:25:09,040
When we continue, a weapons-laden version

420
00:25:09,041 --> 00:25:12,840
of a classic English sports car becomes known

421
00:25:12,841 --> 00:25:14,740
as the ultimate spy toy.

422
00:25:14,741 --> 00:25:19,740
During the making of The World Is Not Enough,

423
00:25:19,840 --> 00:25:22,740
filmmakers discovered by accident that the Q boat

424
00:25:22,840 --> 00:25:25,680
had so much power that its engines could literally

425
00:25:25,780 --> 00:25:28,680
force the bow of the boat under the water.

426
00:25:28,780 --> 00:25:31,620
The move was written into the film.

427
00:25:31,720 --> 00:25:35,620
James Bond Gadgets will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

428
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:44,740
We now return to James Bond Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

429
00:25:44,741 --> 00:25:49,740
The cars used in spy movies have always captivated audiences.

430
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:53,340
They're fast, they're sexy, and they're

431
00:25:53,440 --> 00:25:55,380
loaded with the most outrageous gadgets

432
00:25:55,480 --> 00:25:57,720
filmmakers can think of.

433
00:25:57,820 --> 00:26:00,360
Ask any Bond fan what the greatest gadget from the films

434
00:26:00,460 --> 00:26:03,260
are, and the answer is almost universal.

435
00:26:03,360 --> 00:26:09,260
The Aston Martin DB5, the James Bond car from Goldfinger.

436
00:26:09,360 --> 00:26:11,860
People just blew their minds when they saw this vehicle,

437
00:26:11,861 --> 00:26:14,600
So much so that today, people acknowledge it

438
00:26:14,700 --> 00:26:16,100
as the most famous car in the world.

439
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:19,160
And anybody on the street will see a silver birch Aston

440
00:26:19,260 --> 00:26:23,800
Martin, and they'll say straight away, god, James Bond's car.

441
00:26:23,900 --> 00:26:27,380
For Rocky Santiago, seeing the movie as a teenager

442
00:26:27,480 --> 00:26:31,540
would plant the seeds of a lifelong love of the Aston

443
00:26:31,640 --> 00:26:32,420
Martin.

444
00:26:32,520 --> 00:26:35,380
It took him 21 years to find one.

445
00:26:35,480 --> 00:26:39,980
But now he owns a pristine 1965 model of the car.

446
00:26:39,981 --> 00:26:43,180
though his is red, not silver.

447
00:26:43,280 --> 00:26:46,620
Well, my introduction to Bond was the movie Goldfinger.

448
00:26:46,720 --> 00:26:49,420
I was in high school, and that's an impressionable age.

449
00:26:49,520 --> 00:26:54,400
And when you saw Sean Connery driving around in his DB5,

450
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:59,100
it left an unforgettable memory.

451
00:26:59,200 --> 00:27:01,360
Out on the road, Santiago says the car

452
00:27:01,460 --> 00:27:03,340
is an immediate attention grabber.

453
00:27:05,840 --> 00:27:09,280
You can go to any car show anywhere on the planet

454
00:27:09,281 --> 00:27:14,280
And people that maybe wouldn't have given you the time of day

455
00:27:14,380 --> 00:27:17,520
all of a sudden are interested in you

456
00:27:17,620 --> 00:27:20,720
because you own and drive a DB5.

457
00:27:20,820 --> 00:27:22,920
And I know it sounds weird, but it's true

458
00:27:23,020 --> 00:27:25,760
because it's happened to me over and over again.

459
00:27:25,860 --> 00:27:27,740
I think the car is special.

460
00:27:27,840 --> 00:27:30,800
I think people that don't even like sports cars

461
00:27:30,900 --> 00:27:32,040
know what they are.

462
00:27:32,140 --> 00:27:35,800
They always refer to them as the type of car that Bond drives.

463
00:27:39,280 --> 00:27:42,580
The vehicles of James Bond are some of the most famous

464
00:27:42,680 --> 00:27:44,680
in the world.

465
00:27:44,780 --> 00:27:48,020
But outside the confines of the silver screen,

466
00:27:48,120 --> 00:27:51,300
they're almost never seen in public.

467
00:27:51,400 --> 00:27:55,500
From April to December 2001, Bond fans

468
00:27:55,600 --> 00:27:59,340
had a chance to see half a dozen of the actual movie cars,

469
00:27:59,440 --> 00:28:03,340
which are kept in the archives collection of Eon Productions,

470
00:28:03,440 --> 00:28:07,040
producers of the Bond films.

471
00:28:07,041 --> 00:28:10,640
It happened two hours south of London at England's National

472
00:28:10,740 --> 00:28:14,280
Motor Museum at Beaulieu.

473
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:19,280
Margaret Rowles is an administrator with the museum.

474
00:28:19,380 --> 00:28:21,180
On a recent tour of the display, she

475
00:28:21,280 --> 00:28:23,420
noted in typical British understatement

476
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:29,700
that it was a shame that a $225,000 BMW Z8 was sawed in half

477
00:28:29,800 --> 00:28:33,140
during the filming, the world is not enough.

478
00:28:37,040 --> 00:28:41,200
Yes, a bit of a disaster.

479
00:28:41,201 --> 00:28:42,880
I wouldn't want it to happen to mine.

480
00:28:43,260 --> 00:28:45,480
And what a shame to do this to such a pretty vehicle.

481
00:28:46,120 --> 00:28:50,220
It was used in a very spectacular stunt in the film,

482
00:28:50,400 --> 00:28:55,120
and it was literally cut in half by these five giant circular saws

483
00:28:55,121 --> 00:28:57,180
that were suspended from a helicopter.

484
00:28:57,740 --> 00:29:01,240
Some serious damage, but, of course, Bond survived.

485
00:29:02,420 --> 00:29:06,660
You can imagine BMW, when we went to their development factory

486
00:29:06,661 --> 00:29:07,920
in Munich.

487
00:29:08,020 --> 00:29:10,660
The Z8 was there as the new car, you know,

488
00:29:10,760 --> 00:29:14,740
and they pulled back the covers, and there was this black beast

489
00:29:14,840 --> 00:29:16,300
there, you know, with red upholstery.

490
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:20,480
And we all went over and walked around and touched it.

491
00:29:20,580 --> 00:29:23,080
And of course, you can see the look of dismay when we said,

492
00:29:23,180 --> 00:29:24,080
can we cut one in half?

493
00:29:26,880 --> 00:29:29,480
The exhibit has a sampling of Bond vehicles

494
00:29:29,580 --> 00:29:33,520
from throughout 007's movie career.

495
00:29:33,620 --> 00:29:36,120
This is the Lotus Esprit from The Spy Who Loved Me.

496
00:29:36,121 --> 00:29:39,080
And the thing you'll notice is that it hasn't got any wheels.

497
00:29:39,081 --> 00:29:40,720
It's got fins instead.

498
00:29:40,721 --> 00:29:44,120
It was an amphibious car, and it went underwater.

499
00:29:44,121 --> 00:29:46,520
It also had a periscope in the roof

500
00:29:46,521 --> 00:29:49,640
so that Bond could actually see his adversaries

501
00:29:49,641 --> 00:29:52,200
when he was under the water.

502
00:29:52,201 --> 00:29:56,600
This is a BMW 750 from the film Tomorrow Never Dies,

503
00:29:56,601 --> 00:29:59,480
and it is the ultimate Bond gadget car.

504
00:29:59,481 --> 00:30:02,680
It has everything conceivable that you could want on it,

505
00:30:02,681 --> 00:30:06,280
including this magnificent row of rockets coming out

506
00:30:06,281 --> 00:30:12,080
of the sunroof, and a wonderful 20,000 volt defense system

507
00:30:12,081 --> 00:30:14,560
to deter any would-be car thief.

508
00:30:14,561 --> 00:30:17,060
Now, wouldn't we all like that on our cars?

509
00:30:17,061 --> 00:30:19,860
